<html>

<head>
    <title>photo archive</title>
    <link href="style.css" rel="stylesheet">
</head>

<body>
    {% include "partial-nav.njk" %}

    <div class="about">

        <h1 >About </h1>

        <div class="about-section">
            <p>Photo Orphange is a digital space in which lost photos gather.</p>
        </div>

        <div class="about-section">
            <h2>How to contribute</h2>
            <p>Simply submit a photo that you find in the wild and leave it a message. It doesn't whether that be one that randomly sits around a photobooth, hanged on a cafe's wall, dug up from a dusty corner in a vintage shop.</p>
        </div>

        <div class="about-section">
            <h2>Inspiration</h2>
            <p>
                We tend to picture old photographs as rare, black and white, and a little mysterious, things kept behind glass. Online
                archives often make them feel even more distant, with every photo carefully labeled, dated and filed away. But a photo
                doesn't need a catalog entry to matter. It just needs someone to look at it.
            </p>
        </div>

        <div class="about-section">
            <p><a href="/form">Add a photo</a></p>
        </div>

    </div>
</body>

</html>